  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'schemabench_0052' consists of 11 tables. The primary purpose of this schema is to manage various aspects of a social media platform, such as user accounts, content creation, interactions, notifications, and more.\nThe 'failed_jobs' table is used to store information about jobs that have failed during the execution. It contains columns for the job's id, connection details, queue, payload, exception details, and the timestamp of when the job failed.\nThe 'likes' table represents user likes on different types of content, such as replies and threads. It has columns for the like's id, the id of the user who liked, the id of the liked content, the type of the liked content, and timestamps for when the like was created and updated.\nThe 'migrations' table is used to track the database schema migrations. It contains columns for the migration's id and the batch number.\nThe 'notifications' table stores information about notifications sent to users. It has columns for the notification's id, type, the type of the notifiable entity, the id of the notifiable entity, the data of the notification, the timestamp of when the notification was read, and timestamps for when the notification was created and updated.\nThe 'password_resets' table is used to handle password reset requests. It contains columns for the user's email and a reset token.\nThe 'replies' table represents user replies to different types of content. It has columns for the reply's id, the body of the reply, the id of the reply's author, the id of the replyable content, and timestamps for when the reply was created and updated.\nThe 'subscriptions' table manages user subscriptions to different types of content. It has columns for the subscription's id, the id of the user who subscribed, the id of the subscriptionable content, the type of the subscriptionable content, and timestamps for when the subscription was created and updated.\nThe 'taggables' table is used to tag different types of content. It has columns for the taggable's id, the id of the taggable content, the id of the tag, and timestamps for when the tag was created and updated.\nThe 'tags' table stores information about tags. It has columns for the tag's id, the name of the tag, and a slug.\nThe 'threads' table represents user threads. It has columns for the thread's id, the id of the thread's author, the subject of the thread, the body of the thread, a slug, and timestamps for when the thread was created and updated.\nThe 'users' table stores information about users. It has columns for the user's id, their GitHub id and username, their email, name, timestamps for when the user was created and updated, their remember token, a confirmation status, a confirmation code, a username, a password, a type, a bio, and a timestamp of when the user was banned.\nEach table has foreign keys that reference other tables, ensuring referential integrity and maintaining relationships between tables.",
  "gold_question": "Community Portal for Laravel\n\n 1) Background / Context\nThe project involves developing a community portal powered by Laravel.io. This platform functions as a forum for Laravel enthusiasts to facilitate problem-solving, knowledge sharing, and community building. It serves as a hub for discussions, idea sharing, and assistance on Laravel-related topics.\n\n 2) Objectives\n- Provide a seamless forum experience for users to discuss Laravel-related issues.\n- Enable knowledge sharing and community engagement among Laravel enthusiasts.\n- Facilitate user interactions through features like threads, replies, and tags.\n\n 3) Stakeholders & Users\n- Laravel Enthusiasts: Primary users who participate in discussions and knowledge sharing.\n- Administrators: Manage the platform, oversee user interactions, and ensure smooth operation.\n\n 4) Functional Descriptions\n- User Registration: Users can sign up using their email or GitHub credentials.\n- Threads: Users can create threads to initiate discussions or queries.\n- Replies: Users can post replies to threads or as responses to other replies.\n- Likes: Users can like threads and replies to show appreciation or agreement.\n- Notifications: Users receive notifications related to interactions on their posts or threads they follow.\n- Tags: Threads can be tagged for categorization and improved searchability.\n- Subscriptions: Users can subscribe to threads or topics to receive updates.\n- Password Resets: Users can reset their passwords using a secure token-based system.\n\n 5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n- Scalability: The system should efficiently handle a growing number of users and posts.\n- Security: Secure authentication and authorization, with provisions for banning users when necessary.\n- Performance: The system should ensure responsive interactions and fast loading times.\n- Reliability: Provide consistent uptime and handle failed job processes effectively.\n- Usability: User-friendly interfaces for ease of use, even for those not technically proficient.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0052.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0052\nCREATE TABLE `failed_jobs` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `connection` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `queue` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `payload` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `exception` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `failed_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `likes` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `content_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `content_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `likes_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `migrations` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `batch` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `notifications` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `notifiable_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `notifiable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `data` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `read_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `password_resets` (\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`email`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `replies` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `body` text NOT NULL,\n  `author_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `replyable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `replyable_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `replies_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`author_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `subscriptions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `subscriptionable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `subscriptionable_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `subscriptions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `taggables` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `taggable_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `taggable_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `tag_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `taggables_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`tag_id`) REFERENCES `tags` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `tags` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `threads` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `author_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `subject` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `body` text NOT NULL,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `threads_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`author_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `github_id` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `github_username` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `remember_token` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `confirmed` tinyint(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `confirmation_code` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `bio` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `banned_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
